    "answer": {
      "short": "Mary is right. The cubic graphs are not reflections in either coordinate axis.\n\nReplacing $x$ by $-x$ in $y=(x-1)^3$ gives $y=-(x+1)^3$, whereas the even power in the quadratic cancels the negative factor.",
      "worked": "Reflect the quadratic in the $y$-axis by replacing $x$ by $-x$:\n$$\\begin{align*}y&=(-x-1)^2\\\\y&=[-(x+1)]^2\\\\y&=(x+1)^2\\end{align*}$$\nFor the cubic:\n$$\\begin{align*}y&=(-x-1)^3\\\\y&=[-(x+1)]^3\\\\y&=-(x+1)^3\\end{align*}$$\nThe even power removes the negative factor; the odd power retains it. An $x$-axis reflection instead gives $y=-(x-1)^3$, also different from $y=(x+1)^3$. Mary is right: the cubic graphs are not reflections in either coordinate axis.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
